  "sections": {
    "work": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The lolling head, the hanging arm, the swollen, spread feet depict a raw and realistic view of the Virgin's mortal remains.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Death of the Virgin (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_the_Virgin_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The theatrical drape of blood-red cloth looms in the upper portion of the canvas; a common motif in deposition painting, here used to heighten the scene's dramatic effect.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Death of the Virgin (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_the_Virgin_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Giovanni Baglione, another contemporary, tells that it was due to Mary's bare legs—a matter of decorum in either case.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Caravaggio",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caravaggio",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Caravaggio's painting is the last major Catholic work of art in which Mary is clearly dead.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Death of the Virgin (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_the_Virgin_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "matters": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Giulio Mancini thought Caravaggio modelled a prostitute, possibly his mistress, as the Virgin.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Death of the Virgin (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_the_Virgin_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Upon the recommendation by Peter Paul Rubens, who praised it as one of Caravaggio's best works, the painting was bought by Vincenzo Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Death of the Virgin (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_the_Virgin_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "However, Caravaggio had fled Rome by then, never to publicly return.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Death of the Virgin (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_of_the_Virgin_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
